IMPORT prelude

-- Spam Act 2003 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- the marketing messages that reach me, and whether I can stop them.
--
-- SEAT: the consumer receiving marketing messages (the relevant electronic account-holder) -- "Is this message spam under
-- the Act? Did the sender need my consent? Must it tell me who sent it and let me unsubscribe? How soon must my unsubscribe
-- be honoured?". Every exported question is that person's, except the two in § Part 4 and Schedule 3, which are asked from
-- the seat of the sender (a small business such as Daniel's company) as far as "What can my company be made to pay?".
-- Where the Act hands a judgment to someone else ("it would be concluded" that a purpose is commercial; consent that "can
-- reasonably be inferred"; the sender's mistake or ignorance of the Australian link), it is asked as a fact, and the
-- answer is conditional on it.
--
-- Provisions covered: s 4 (relevant electronic account-holder, as the person asking); s 5(1), (5) (electronic message; voice
-- calls excluded); s 6(1)(d)-(o) (commercial electronic message); s 7(a)-(d) (Australian link); s 16(1)-(5) (unsolicited
-- commercial electronic messages); s 17(1) (sender information); s 18(1), (3), (9) (functional unsubscribe facility);
-- s 25(3)-(6) (maximum civil penalties); Schedule 1 cll 2-4 (designated commercial electronic messages); Schedule 2 cll 2,
-- 4, 6 (consent: express, inferred, conspicuous publication, withdrawal after 5 business days); Schedule 3 cl 5 (infringement
-- notice amounts).
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. as at register id C2016C00614 (latest amendments listed in the endnotes: 11 Feb 2016), as
-- deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A01214.txt (retrieved 13 October 2026).
-- NOT DEPOSITED: the Spam Regulations 2021 (any purpose added by s 6(1)(p); messages excluded by s 6(7); conditions under
-- ss 17(1)(c), 18(1)(g) and Sch 1 cl 2(1)(c); further designated messages, Sch 1 cl 5; circumstances in which consent may or
-- may not be inferred, Sch 2 cl 5). Those are not encoded: each answer here is as if the regulations added nothing. The
-- dollar value of a penalty unit (Crimes Act 1914 s 4AA) is not deposited; penalties are given in penalty units.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: s 7(e) (undeliverable messages); s 16(6) (messages to non-existent addresses) and the ancillary contraventions
-- in ss 16(9), 17(5), 18(6); Part 3 (address-harvesting software); s 24 (the court's discretion within the maximum), ss 26-29
-- (ACMA proceedings, compensation and benefit orders), Parts 5 and 6 (injunctions, undertakings), Part 7 (ACMA functions,
-- warnings); Schedule 3 other than cl 5; Sch 2 cl 3 (another user of my account consenting for me) beyond treating a consent
-- or unsubscribe sent from my account as mine; public holidays in the 5-business-day count. The Act gives the consumer no
-- right to sue: proceedings for a penalty are the ACMA's (s 26(1)). Marketing voice calls are the Do Not Call Register Act
-- 2006, not this Act (s 5(5)).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Spam Act 2003 -- from the seat of the consumer receiving marketing messages`

-- Sch 1 cl 3: a message authorised by "(i) a government body; (ii) a registered political party; (iii) a registered
-- charity"; cl 4: by "an educational institution". Everyone else is one kind for this Act.
DECLARE `Sender kind` IS ONE OF
    `a business or other sender`
    `a government body`
    `a registered political party`
    `a registered charity`
    `an educational institution`

-- One message as it reached me. Every field is something I can see in the message or say about myself, except the two
-- judgments flagged below.
DECLARE Message HAS
    `was a voice call over a standard telephone service` IS A BOOLEAN
    `was sent over the internet or another listed carriage service` IS A BOOLEAN
    `was sent to my email, instant messaging, telephone or similar account` IS A BOOLEAN
    `offers or promotes goods or services, or a supplier of them` IS A BOOLEAN
    `offers or promotes land or an interest in land, or a supplier of it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `offers or promotes a business or investment opportunity, or a provider of one` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is meant to help someone dishonestly get property, a financial advantage or a gain from another` IS A BOOLEAN
    `originated in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the sender was in Australia, or managed and controlled from Australia, when it was sent` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the device I accessed it on was in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I was in Australia when I accessed it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the sender` IS A `Sender kind`
    `is no more than factual information, with directly-related comment and the sender's details` IS A BOOLEAN
    `would not be commercial without the sender's details` IS A BOOLEAN
    `relates to goods or services the sender itself supplies or offers` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I or a member of my household is or was enrolled at the sending institution` IS A BOOLEAN
    `clearly and accurately identifies who authorised it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `gives accurate contact details likely to stay valid for 30 days` IS A BOOLEAN
    `clearly and conspicuously says I may unsubscribe to an electronic address in it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `that address is likely to take unsubscribe messages for at least 30 days` IS A BOOLEAN
    `that address was legitimately obtained` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a contract between me and the sender is inconsistent with an unsubscribe facility` IS A BOOLEAN

-- What I have said to, or done with, the sender. Two fields are judgments (flagged).
DECLARE `My dealings with the sender` HAS
    `I expressly consented` IS A BOOLEAN
    `consent can reasonably be inferred from my conduct and my relationships with the sender` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the only ground for inferring it is that my address was published` IS A BOOLEAN
    `my work address was conspicuously published with my agreement and without a no-spam statement` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the message is relevant to my work, office or role` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I sent the sender an unsubscribe message` IS A BOOLEAN
    `business days completed since I sent it, counting the day I sent it` IS A NUMBER

§§ `ss 5, 6, 7 -- is this a commercial electronic message with an Australian link`

-- s 5(1): "an electronic message is a message sent: (a) using: (i) an internet carriage service; or (ii) any other listed
-- carriage service; and (b) to an electronic address in connection with: (i) an email account; or (ii) an instant messaging
-- account; or (iii) a telephone account; or (iv) a similar account."
-- s 5(5): "If a message is sent by way of a voice call made using a standard telephone service, the message is not an
-- electronic message for the purposes of this Act." (A text message, an email, a direct message: electronic messages. A
-- telemarketing call on an ordinary phone line: not.)
@export Is this message an electronic message under the Spam Act (s 5)? A voice call on an ordinary phone service is not.
GIVEN `the message` IS A Message @desc The message you received
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this an electronic message` IF
        (NOT `the message`'s `was a voice call over a standard telephone service`)
    AND `the message`'s `was sent over the internet or another listed carriage service`
    AND `the message`'s `was sent to my email, instant messaging, telephone or similar account`

-- s 6(1): a commercial electronic message is an electronic message where, having regard to its content, presentation and
-- linked content, "it would be concluded that the purpose, or one of the purposes, of the message is: (d) to offer to
-- supply goods or services; or (e) to advertise or promote goods or services; or (f) to advertise or promote a supplier,
-- or prospective supplier, of goods or services"; (g)-(i) the same for land or an interest in land; (j)-(l) the same for
-- "a business opportunity or investment opportunity"; "(m) to assist or enable a person, by a deception, to dishonestly
-- obtain property belonging to another person; or (n) ... a financial advantage ...; or (o) to assist or enable a person
-- to dishonestly obtain a gain from another person". Each purpose flag is the conclusion "it would be concluded", asked
-- as a fact. (p) and s 6(7) (regulations) are not encoded.
@export Is this a commercial electronic message -- one whose purpose, or one of whose purposes, is to sell, promote or scam (s 6)?
GIVEN `the message` IS A Message @desc The message you received
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this a commercial electronic message` IF
        `Is this an electronic message` `the message`
    AND (   `the message`'s `offers or promotes goods or services, or a supplier of them`
         OR `the message`'s `offers or promotes land or an interest in land, or a supplier of it`
         OR `the message`'s `offers or promotes a business or investment opportunity, or a provider of one`
         OR `the message`'s `is meant to help someone dishonestly get property, a financial advantage or a gain from another`)

-- s 7: an Australian link "if, and only if: (a) the message originates in Australia; or (b) the individual or
-- organisation who sent the message, or authorised the sending of the message, is: (i) an individual who is physically
-- present in Australia when the message is sent; or (ii) an organisation whose central management and control is in
-- Australia when the message is sent; or (c) the computer, server or device that is used to access the message is located
-- in Australia; or (d) the relevant electronic account-holder is: (i) an individual who is physically present in Australia
-- when the message is accessed". (e), undeliverable messages, is not encoded.
@export Does this message have an Australian link (s 7)?
GIVEN `the message` IS A Message @desc The message you received
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does it have an Australian link` IF
        `the message`'s `originated in Australia`
    OR  `the message`'s `the sender was in Australia, or managed and controlled from Australia, when it was sent`
    OR  `the message`'s `the device I accessed it on was in Australia`
    OR  `the message`'s `I was in Australia when I accessed it`

§§ `Schedule 1 -- designated commercial electronic messages`

-- Sch 1 cl 2(1): designated if "(a) the message consists of no more than factual information (with or without
-- directly-related comment) and any or all of the following additional information:" the name, logo and contact details of
-- the sender, author, employer, partnership, organisation or sponsor, and the s 17 and s 18 information; "and (b) assuming
-- that none of that additional information had been included in the message, the message would not have been a commercial
-- electronic message".
-- cl 3: designated if "(a) the sending of the message is authorised by any of the following bodies: (i) a government body;
-- (ii) a registered political party; (iii) a registered charity; and (b) the message relates to goods or services; and (c)
-- the body is the supplier, or prospective supplier, of the goods or services concerned".
-- cl 4: designated if authorised by an educational institution, "(i) the relevant electronic account-holder is, or has been,
-- enrolled as a student in that institution; (ii) a member or former member of the household of the relevant electronic
-- account-holder is, or has been, enrolled", and it relates to goods or services the institution supplies.
-- cl 1 Note 2: designated messages "are exempt from section 16 ... and section 18"; Note 1: they "must comply with section 17".
@export Is this a designated commercial electronic message -- factual information, or from a government body, registered party, registered charity, or my (or my household's) school or university about what it supplies (Sch 1)?
GIVEN `the message` IS A Message @desc The message you received
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it a designated commercial electronic message` IF
        `Is this an electronic message` `the message`
    AND (   (    `the message`'s `is no more than factual information, with directly-related comment and the sender's details`
             AND `the message`'s `would not be commercial without the sender's details`)
         OR (    (   `the message`'s `the sender` EQUALS `a government body`
                  OR `the message`'s `the sender` EQUALS `a registered political party`
                  OR `the message`'s `the sender` EQUALS `a registered charity`)
             AND `the message`'s `relates to goods or services the sender itself supplies or offers`)
         OR (    `the message`'s `the sender` EQUALS `an educational institution`
             AND `the message`'s `I or a member of my household is or was enrolled at the sending institution`
             AND `the message`'s `relates to goods or services the sender itself supplies or offers`))

§§ `Schedule 2 -- consent, and withdrawing it`

-- Sch 2 cl 2: "consent means: (a) express consent; or (b) consent that can reasonably be inferred from: (i) the conduct;
-- and (ii) the business and other relationships; of the individual or organisation concerned." The inference is a
-- judgment, asked as a fact.
-- cl 4(1): consent "may not be inferred from the mere fact that the relevant electronic address has been published".
-- cl 4(2): but where a work address (of an employee, director, officer, partner, office-holder or self-employed person, or
-- of an office, position, function or role) "has been conspicuously published", it is reasonable to assume publication was
-- agreed, and it is "not accompanied by" a statement that the holder "does not want to receive unsolicited commercial
-- electronic messages at that electronic address", the holder "is taken ... to have consented ... so long as the messages
-- are relevant to" the work-related business, functions or duties, the office or position, or the function or role.
-- cl 6(1): after I send the sender "a message to the effect that the account-holder does not want to receive any further
-- commercial electronic messages at that electronic address from or authorised by that individual or organisation", "the
-- withdrawal of consent takes effect at the end of the period of 5 business days beginning on" the day an electronic
-- message was sent (by post: the day of service; otherwise: the day it was delivered). cl 6(2): "a business day is a day
-- that is not a Saturday, a Sunday or a public holiday" in the place the message was sent or delivered to.
`the number of business days before a withdrawal takes effect` MEANS 5

@export Has my unsubscribe (withdrawal of consent) taken effect -- have 5 business days, starting with the day I sent it, ended (Sch 2 cl 6)?
GIVEN `business days completed` IS A NUMBER @desc Business days that have ended since you sent your unsubscribe message, counting the day you sent it if it was a business day
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has my unsubscribe taken effect` IF
    `business days completed` AT LEAST `the number of business days before a withdrawal takes effect`

@export Did I consent to this message -- expressly, by inference from my conduct and relationships, or by publishing my work address -- and not withdraw it (Sch 2)?
GIVEN `my dealings` IS A `My dealings with the sender` @desc What you have said to, or done with, the sender
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Did I consent to this message` IF
        (   `my dealings`'s `I expressly consented`
         OR (    `my dealings`'s `consent can reasonably be inferred from my conduct and my relationships with the sender`
             AND (NOT `my dealings`'s `the only ground for inferring it is that my address was published`))
         OR (    `my dealings`'s `my work address was conspicuously published with my agreement and without a no-spam statement`
             AND `my dealings`'s `the message is relevant to my work, office or role`))
    AND (NOT (    `my dealings`'s `I sent the sender an unsubscribe message`
              AND `Has my unsubscribe taken effect` (`my dealings`'s `business days completed since I sent it, counting the day I sent it`)))

-- The encoder's table, not the Act's: with no public holiday, the number of calendar days from the day I send an
-- electronic unsubscribe message (counted as day 1) to the last day on which the sender may still rely on my old consent.
-- Sent Monday: Mon-Fri, 5 days. Sent Tuesday: Tue-Fri and Mon, 7 days. Sent Saturday: the period begins on Saturday but its
-- first business day is Monday, so to Friday, 7 days. Sent Sunday: to Friday, 6 days.
DECLARE `Day of the week` IS ONE OF
    Monday
    Tuesday
    Wednesday
    Thursday
    Friday
    Saturday
    Sunday

@export If I unsubscribe by electronic message on this day of the week, how many calendar days (counting that day) may the sender still rely on my old consent, assuming no public holidays (Sch 2 cl 6)?
GIVEN `the day I send it` IS A `Day of the week` @desc The day of the week on which you send your unsubscribe message
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many calendar days until my unsubscribe takes effect` IS
    CONSIDER `the day I send it`
    WHEN Monday THEN 5
    WHEN Sunday THEN 6
    OTHERWISE 7

§§ `s 16 -- did the sender need my consent`

-- s 16(1): "A person must not send, or cause to be sent, a commercial electronic message that: (a) has an Australian link;
-- and (b) is not a designated commercial electronic message." (2): "Subsection (1) does not apply if the relevant
-- electronic account-holder consented to the sending of the message." (3): nor if the sender "(a) did not know; and (b)
-- could not, with reasonable diligence, have ascertained; that the message had an Australian link". (4): nor if sent "by
-- mistake". (5): the sender "bears an evidential burden". (3) and (4) are facts only the sender can show, so the answer
-- is conditional on them.
DECLARE `Consent answer` IS ONE OF
    `the Spam Act does not reach it, because it is not an electronic message`
    `no consent needed, because it is not a commercial electronic message`
    `no consent needed, because it has no Australian link`
    `no consent needed, because it is a designated commercial electronic message`
    `it was allowed, because I consented`
    `it breaks s 16(1), unless the sender shows it was sent by mistake or it could not have known of the Australian link`

@export Did the sender need my consent to send me this message, and was it sent without it (s 16)?
GIVEN `the message` IS A Message @desc The message you received
      `my dealings` IS A `My dealings with the sender` @desc What you have said to, or done with, the sender
GIVETH A `Consent answer`
DECIDE `Did the sender need my consent` IS
    IF NOT `Is this an electronic message` `the message`
    THEN `the Spam Act does not reach it, because it is not an electronic message`
    ELSE IF NOT `Is this a commercial electronic message` `the message`
         THEN `no consent needed, because it is not a commercial electronic message`
         ELSE IF NOT `Does it have an Australian link` `the message`
              THEN `no consent needed, because it has no Australian link`
              ELSE IF `Is it a designated commercial electronic message` `the message`
                   THEN `no consent needed, because it is a designated commercial electronic message`
                   ELSE IF `Did I consent to this message` `my dealings`
                        THEN `it was allowed, because I consented`
                        ELSE `it breaks s 16(1), unless the sender shows it was sent by mistake or it could not have known of the Australian link`

§§ `s 17 -- must it say who sent it`

-- s 17(1): a commercial electronic message with an Australian link must not be sent "unless: (a) the message clearly and
-- accurately identifies the individual or organisation who authorised the sending of the message; and (b) the message
-- includes accurate information about how the recipient can readily contact that individual or organisation; and (c) ...
-- the regulations; and (d) that information is reasonably likely to be valid for at least 30 days after the message is
-- sent". Designated messages are not exempt (Sch 1 cl 1 Note 1). (2)-(4): the same defences as s 16(3)-(5).
@export Must this message identify who authorised it and how I can contact them (s 17)? Designated messages must too.
GIVEN `the message` IS A Message @desc The message you received
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must it identify the sender` IF
        `Is this a commercial electronic message` `the message`
    AND `Does it have an Australian link` `the message`

@export Does this message break the sender-information rule -- it had to identify the sender and give contact details valid for 30 days, and did not (s 17(1))?
GIVEN `the message` IS A Message @desc The message you received
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does it break the sender-information rule` IF
        `Must it identify the sender` `the message`
    AND (NOT (    `the message`'s `clearly and accurately identifies who authorised it`
              AND `the message`'s `gives accurate contact details likely to stay valid for 30 days`))

§§ `s 18 -- must it let me unsubscribe`

-- s 18(1): a commercial electronic message that "(a) has an Australian link; and (b) is not a designated commercial
-- electronic message" must not be sent "unless: (c) the message includes: (i) a statement to the effect that the recipient
-- may use an electronic address set out in the message to send an unsubscribe message to the individual or organisation
-- who authorised the sending of the first-mentioned message; or (ii) a statement to similar effect; and (d) the statement
-- is presented in a clear and conspicuous manner; and (e) the electronic address is reasonably likely to be capable of
-- receiving" my unsubscribe message and a reasonable number of others "at all times during a period of at least 30 days
-- after the message is sent; and (f) the electronic address is legitimately obtained; and (g)" the regulations.
-- s 18(3): it "does not apply to the extent (if any) to which it is inconsistent with the terms of a contract or other
-- agreement between" the sender and me. s 18(9): an unsubscribe message is one "to the effect that the relevant electronic
-- account-holder does not want to receive any further commercial electronic messages from or authorised by that
-- individual or organisation".
@export Must this message contain a functional unsubscribe facility (s 18)? Designated messages need not.
GIVEN `the message` IS A Message @desc The message you received
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must it let me unsubscribe` IF
        `Is this a commercial electronic message` `the message`
    AND `Does it have an Australian link` `the message`
    AND (NOT `Is it a designated commercial electronic message` `the message`)
    AND (NOT `the message`'s `a contract between me and the sender is inconsistent with an unsubscribe facility`)

@export Does this message break the unsubscribe rule -- it needed a clear unsubscribe statement and a working, legitimately obtained address for 30 days, and lacked one (s 18(1))?
GIVEN `the message` IS A Message @desc The message you received
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does it break the unsubscribe rule` IF
        `Must it let me unsubscribe` `the message`
    AND (NOT (    `the message`'s `clearly and conspicuously says I may unsubscribe to an electronic address in it`
              AND `the message`'s `that address is likely to take unsubscribe messages for at least 30 days`
              AND `the message`'s `that address was legitimately obtained`))

§§ `s 25 and Schedule 3 -- what the sender can be made to pay (from the sender's seat)`

-- s 25(3)-(6): the maximum penalty for each contravention, in penalty units:
--   body corporate, no prior record: "if the civil penalty provision is subsection 16(1), (6) or (9)--100 penalty units; or
--   (ii) in any other case--50 penalty units"; a person other than a body corporate, no prior record: 20 or 10; body
--   corporate, prior record: 500 or 250; other person, prior record: 100 or 50.
-- And for 2 or more contraventions on one day, the total must not exceed (same order): 2,000 or 1,000; 400 or 200; 10,000 or
-- 5,000; 2,000 or 1,000. s 25(2): a prior record means an earlier s 24(1) order on the same civil penalty provision.
DECLARE `Spam rule` IS ONE OF
    `s 16, unsolicited messages`
    `s 17, sender information`
    `s 18, unsubscribe facility`

DECLARE `Penalty limits` HAS
    `per contravention` IS A NUMBER
    `for all contraventions on one day` IS A NUMBER

GIVEN `the rule` IS A `Spam rule`
      `a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a prior record` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Penalty limits`
`the s 25 limits` MEANS
    IF `a body corporate`
    THEN IF `a prior record`
         THEN IF `the rule` EQUALS `s 16, unsolicited messages` THEN `Penalty limits` 500 10000 ELSE `Penalty limits` 250 5000
         ELSE IF `the rule` EQUALS `s 16, unsolicited messages` THEN `Penalty limits` 100 2000 ELSE `Penalty limits` 50 1000
    ELSE IF `a prior record`
         THEN IF `the rule` EQUALS `s 16, unsolicited messages` THEN `Penalty limits` 100 2000 ELSE `Penalty limits` 50 1000
         ELSE IF `the rule` EQUALS `s 16, unsolicited messages` THEN `Penalty limits` 20 400 ELSE `Penalty limits` 10 200

@export As a sender, what is the most the Federal Court can order me (or my company) to pay for one contravention of this rule, in penalty units (s 25)?
GIVEN `the rule` IS A `Spam rule` @desc The rule broken
      `a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the sender is a body corporate (a company)
      `a prior record` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has already made a penalty order against the sender for this rule
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be made to pay per contravention` IS
    (`the s 25 limits` `the rule` `a body corporate` `a prior record`)'s `per contravention`

@export As a sender, what is the most the Federal Court can order me (or my company) to pay in total for 2 or more contraventions of this rule on one day, in penalty units (s 25)?
GIVEN `the rule` IS A `Spam rule` @desc The rule broken
      `a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the sender is a body corporate (a company)
      `a prior record` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has already made a penalty order against the sender for this rule
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be made to pay for one day` IS
    (`the s 25 limits` `the rule` `a body corporate` `a prior record`)'s `for all contraventions on one day`

-- Sch 3 cl 5(1), body corporate: s 16 contraventions -- "single" 20; "more than 1, but fewer than 50" 20 times the number;
-- "50 or more" 1,000. Other provisions: 10; 10 times the number; 500. cl 5(2), other persons: s 16 -- 4; 4 times; 200.
-- Others: 2; 2 times; 100. (A single contravention is 1 times the unit rate, so items 1-2 and 4-5 are one formula.)
@export As a sender, how many penalty units must an ACMA infringement notice for this many contraventions of this rule on one day specify (Sch 3 cl 5)?
GIVEN `the rule` IS A `Spam rule` @desc The rule allegedly broken
      `a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the sender is a body corporate (a company)
      `contraventions` IS A NUMBER @desc How many alleged contraventions the notice covers (at least 1)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many penalty units is the infringement notice` IS
    IF `contraventions` AT LEAST 50
    THEN `rate` TIMES 50
    ELSE `rate` TIMES `contraventions`
    WHERE
        `rate` MEANS
            IF `a body corporate`
            THEN IF `the rule` EQUALS `s 16, unsolicited messages` THEN 20 ELSE 10
            ELSE IF `the rule` EQUALS `s 16, unsolicited messages` THEN 4 ELSE 2
